# Task 4 - what happened before what.
#
# Three processes exchange messages. No one shares a clock, so the only
# ordering anyone can know is the one the messages create: if p1 sends to
# p2, that send happened before p2 received it. Everything else is
# concurrent, however it looks on the page.
#
# Each process carries a vector clock with one entry per process. A send
# increments the sender's own entry and carries the stamp along. A
# receive takes the pointwise maximum, then increments its own entry.
#
# Try these in order. Steps 2 and 4 are marked on the lines they belong
# to.
#
#   1. run it and read the diagram: time runs down, messages run across
#   2. add another message and watch which events become ordered
#   3. find two events that are concurrent, where neither stamp
#      dominates the other. This one is a question, not an edit
#   4. make one process slow, and see that order has nothing to do with
#      wall time

# --- the machines ----------------------------------------------------

@process
class Node:
    pass

p1 = Node(speed=1.0)      # step 4: make it 0.3
p2 = Node(speed=1.0)
p3 = Node(speed=1.0)

# --- the world -------------------------------------------------------

world = World(machines=[p1, p2, p3])

# --- the job ---------------------------------------------------------

def story() -> void:
    p1.send(p2, "hello")
    p2.send(p3, "onwards")
    p3.send(p1, "reply")
    # step 2: uncomment, and see what it orders that was not ordered
    # before
    # p2.send(p1, "and again")

job = Calls(run=story)

world.run(job)
